  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">To enhance its support for women-owned businesses, the Federation of Women Entrepreneurs&#x2019; Associations of Nepal (FWEAN) established and launched its Business Development Centre (BDC) in March 2023. This Centre was conceptualized as a one-stop destination for women entrepreneurs to access a diverse array of services essential for their business operations. 

Before embarking on the establishment of the BDC, FWEAN conducted a revision of its strategic plan. The revised strategic plan 2022-26 was adopted by the FWEAN executive committee in December 2022.

Within the BDC, an array services are offered, ranging from digital financial literacy training, marketing assistance, the setup of a Help Desk to aid members in new business registration, legal compliance, financial and HR audits. BDC also introduced the FWEAN mobile app, providing access to digital financial literacy resources, book-keeping and financial reporting tools, a business directory and an Entrepreneurs&#x2019; guide. Specifically, FWEAN conducted a one-day training on digital financial literacy for 50 entrepreneurs in March 2023 and a similar programme for &#x201C;Creative hands of deaf women&#x201D; in April 2023. As of September 2023, BDC services have been used by more than 400 women entrepreneurs, demonstrating its utility, relevance and sustainability. 

Parallel to these initiatives, FWEAN formulated a business plan outlining its strategic trajectory for advancing the BDC&#x2019;s objectives. The ILO supported FWEAN revise its strategic plan. Specifically, the ILO assisted this endeavor by conducting a workshop for 25 of its Board members with technical facilitation on in July 2022.  The outcome of this effort was a revised strategic plan that was formulated by FWEAN facilitated by ILO, which outlined the blueprint for establishing the BDC. 

The ILO supported the establishment of the BDC, in particular the implementation of its various features. Notably, the ILO conducted a special training of trainers for FWEAN members on digital financial literacy and digital marketing in March 2023. To this extent, 35 women entrepreneurs were trained and they in turn have started training other members in the provinces. In addition, the ILO facilitated FWEAN&#x2019;s workshop to formulate a business plan as to how they would proceed to sustain the BDC. The plan provides an outline of all the financial, technical, and strategic aspects, charting the course for the successful and profitable operation of the BDC dedicated to empowering women entrepreneurs in Nepal&#x2019;s business landscape.</narrative>
   </description>
